Bandit Level 2 → Level 3
 Level Goal

The password for the next level is stored in a file called:

--spaces in this filename--

The filename contains spaces, so we need to handle the filename correctly when using Linux commands.

             Step 1 — Check the Files

First, I used the ls command to list the files in the current directory.

ls

The output showed:

--spaces in this filename--
Explanation

ls is used to list the files and directories in the current directory.

Here, I found a file whose name contains multiple spaces:

--spaces in this filename--

              Step 2 — Try to Read the File

I tried to read the file using:

cat "--spaces in this filename--"

The password was displayed successfully.

Explanation

The double quotes " " are used to treat the entire filename as one argument, including the spaces.

Without quotes, Linux may interpret each space-separated part as a separate argument.

For example:

cat --spaces in this filename--

would not correctly represent the filename. 

                Step 3 — Check the Current Directory

I used:

pwd

The output was:

/home/bandit2
Explanation

pwd means Print Working Directory.

It shows the complete path of the directory where I am currently working.

So I was inside:

/home/bandit2

               Step 4 — Understanding the Filename

The filename starts with:

--

and also contains spaces:

--spaces in this filename--

This makes the filename slightly tricky to work with because:

Spaces normally separate arguments in the shell.
A filename beginning with - can sometimes be interpreted as an option by a command.

Therefore, it is safer to explicitly specify the file path.

                Step 5 — Use ./ to Specify the File

I also successfully used:

cat ./--spaces\ in\ this\ filename--
Explanation

./ means:

The current directory.

So:

./--spaces\ in\ this\ filename--

means:

The file named --spaces in this filename-- in the current directory.

The backslash \ is used before each space to tell Linux that the space is part of the filename.

                   Step 6 — Use the Absolute Path

I also used the complete path:

cat /home/bandit2/--spaces\ in\ this\ filename--

This specifies exactly where the file is located.

The command successfully displayed the password.

From this level, I learned:

How to list files using ls.
How to check the current directory using pwd.
How to read files using cat.
How to handle filenames containing spaces.
How to use \ to escape spaces.
How ./ can be used to specify a file in the current directory.
Why filenames beginning with - can require special handling.

A reliable way to access it is:

cat ./--spaces\ in\ this\ filename--

or by using its absolute path:

cat /home/bandit2/--spaces\ in\ this\ filename-- 

